speechtext:
In the interest of presenting an accurate picture of the motion as presented, I feel I should illustrate the difference between closure and time allocation. The Hon. Member for Kamloops-Shuswap is asking me what the difference is. I might ask him to take his amended Standing Orders and refer to page 30, Standing Order 37, which describes closure as follows:
37. Immediately before the Order of the Day for resuming an adjourned debate is called, or if the House be in Committee of the Whole, any Minister of the Crown, who, standing in his or her place, shall have given notice at a previous sitting of his or her intention so to do, may move that the debate shall not be further adjourned, or that the further consideration of any resolution or resolutions, clause or clauses, section or sections, preamble or preambles, title or titles, shall be the first business of the Committee, and shall not further be postponed; and in either case such question shall be decided without debate or amendment;
Therefore, closure is a method of procedure which brings debate to a conclusion and enables the House to secure a decision upon the subject under discussion. Time allocation, however, is something totally different. Again, I would refer the same Member to page 86 of our Standing Orders, Standing Order 82. I draw to his attention exactly what Standing Order 82 says. It reads:
A Minister of the Crown who from his or her place in the House at a previous sitting has stated that an agreement could not be reached under the provisions of Standing Order 80 or 81-
Standing Orders 80 and 81 state that where the three Parties could not agree on an allotted time, then the Minister can use Standing Order 82. I continue:
-in respect of proceedings at the stage at which a public bill was then under consideration either in the House or in any committee and has given notice of his or her intention so to do, may propose a motion for the purpose of allotting a specified number of days or hours for the consideration and disposal of proceedings at that stage;
The key phrase in this Standing Order is:
-may propose a motion for the purpose of allotting a specified number of days or hours for the consideration and disposal of proceedings at that stage;
Time Allocation
Therefore, what we are in fact doing is limiting the time of debate.

The
table below contains the information requested in question 4,642. It should be noted that interest costs are conceptually the same on the Public Accounts and National Accounts bases. The Public Accounts data have been adjusted for consistency with the 1981-82 Public Accounts. These adjustments include:
(a)	treatment of the Post Office as a Crown Corporation throughout the period;
(b)	reclassification from liabilities to assets of notes payable to the International Monetary Fund and other international organizations, and the allocation of special drawing rights;
(c)	treatment of Old Age Security revenues as budgetary revenue and Old Age Security payments as budgetary expenditure throughout the period;
(d)	a reallocation of 1978-79 and 1979-80 Public Accounts provisions for valuation to the individual years for which they actually applied with respect to the following items:
(i)	the government's annual cost with respect to the Unemployment Insurance Account;
(ii)	loans, investments and advances deemed to be non-recoverable;
(iii)	working capital advances;
(iv)	unamortized loan flotation costs of marketable bonds and Canada Savings Bonds; and
(v)	undisbursed balances of appropriations to special accounts;
(e)	the charging to budgetary expenditures of charges made directly to net debt over the period 1958-59 to 1964-65; and
(0 other miscellaneous adjustments to place annual Public Accounts recording on a consistent basis.
The National Accounts data are already publicly available from Statistics Canada but are reprinted here for convenience.
February 7, 1983

